ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Low Back Pain Centralized - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute vertebral crush fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of focal bony point tenderness and absence of significant trauma history.

Acute radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dermatomal distribution of pain, normal reflexes, and intact motor strength.

Renal colic - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is described as burning and superficial rather than deep colicky visceral pain, and urinalysis is clear.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of severe psychomotor agitation and pain crisis, requiring urgent face-to-face evaluation to definitively exclude acute surgical pathologies such as cord compression or fracture. Once structural emergencies were ruled out, the patient required immediate pharmacological intervention targeting neuropathic wind-up, coupled with intensive clinical reassurance to de-escalate the acute panic. Delaying intervention would have resulted in an unnecessary ambulance call-out and ED presentation for a non-surgical centralized pain flare.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered low-dose oral Diazepam 5mg once only to break the acute muscle spasm/panic cycle. Prescribed Amitriptyline 10mg at night for neuropathic modulation. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, LDN, and PEA. Advised cessation of further OTC NSAIDs tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: By utilizing deep diaphragmatic breathing for 5 minutes when pain spikes, you will actively down-regulate your overactive nervous system, directly reducing the perception of burning pain and rewarding yourself with immediate calming relief.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to chronic pain psychology.
DME/Safety Optional: Heat packs recommended for muscular soothing.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to optimize centralized pain pharmacology.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED if there is sudden loss of bowel or bladder control, saddle anesthesia, or sudden inability to bear weight on the legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Centralized pain implies the nervous system is amplifying normal signals; it does not mean new structural damage has occurred tonight.
Clinical Course: Centralized flares are intensely painful but physically safe. With nerve-modulating medication and anxiety reduction, the hypersensitivity will slowly recalibrate to baseline over the coming days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you lose control of your bladder or your legs suddenly give way completely, call 000 immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Standard painkillers fail because this is an issue with the nerve volume being turned up too high; the new nightly medication works by turning down that volume dial in the spinal cord.
Actionable Steps: Shift your focus to pacing your activities tomorrow rather than boom-and-bust; stopping tasks before the pain peaks prevents the nervous system from winding up, rewarding you with more sustained energy throughout the day.

